{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's evolutionary analogy fails to resolve the specific tension in Popper's framework because it conflates 'survival' with 'rational preference.' While the mechanism explains why scientists *do* prefer corroborated theories (biological/methodological survival), it does not justify why they *ought* to do so as a rational act without smuggling in induction. If the environment is unstable (as noted in the target's own counterconditions), the 'fittest' methodology might be one that ignores past tests entirely; thus, appealing to the survival of the current method presupposes that the future will resemble the past regarding methodological efficacy, which is precisely the inductive assumption Popper sought to avoid.","premise":"The target claims preference is a survival trait of the methodology, shifting justification from the theory to the meta-methodology.","premises_essential":["e6bc4f589f051f611665248520ad8a1392b548702378e824fadb80c90bde4a86"],"successor_question":"Does asserting that a methodology has 'survived' implicitly rely on an inductive inference that it will continue to survive or yield truth-like results in the future?","target":"e6bc4f589f051f611665248520ad8a1392b548702378e824fadb80c90bde4a86"}]}